WEBVTT

00:04:27.371 --> 00:04:30.093
between a human being and a mystery,

00:04:30.117 --> 00:04:32.075
a mystery that they wanted to connect to.

00:04:32.099 --> 00:04:35.904
And that mystery is called "inspiration,"
which literally means "the in breath,"

00:04:35.928 --> 00:04:37.896
you know, that something comes to you.

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:39.441
And what I find fascinating is,

00:04:39.465 --> 00:04:44.622
even the most didactically rational,
empirical thinkers in the world,

00:04:44.646 --> 00:04:46.990
when they talk about
where their ideas come from,

00:04:47.014 --> 00:04:49.545
they tend to use the ancient
language of inspiration.

00:04:49.569 --> 00:04:50.729
Even they do.

00:04:50.753 --> 00:04:53.268
They say, "This idea came to me."

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:54.584
That's still how we say it.

00:04:54.608 --> 00:04:56.521
But if you were to nail them down on it,

00:04:56.545 --> 00:04:58.545
they would be like, "No, it came from me."

00:04:58.569 --> 00:05:01.464
And I'm like, "Well, I'm not sure
it didn't come to you."